A||||12||RiceCooker||I Sure Do Miss My Friend (please read)||||9||
Z||000000||RiceCooker||10-28-2000||04:39 PM||||I don't mean to put a damper on Halloween weekend but it feels better to talk about it and I know you guys (and girls) will listen.<P>It will be the second anniversary of Clayton McGloan's death on Halloween.<BR>Clayton was a friend and hockey teammate of mine when his life was taken at a party gone bad. For no apparent reason, he was stabbed and beaten to death by about ten frustrated teenagers when he showed up to meet his girlfriend at a party. He was 17 years old. He never got to graduate, He never got to play junior hockey, and never got a chance to say goodbye to his family or friends. <P>It is for this reason that we should never take the good things in life for granted. For it could have been you or me in the wrong place at the wrong time. <P>Thanks for listening, Todd<P>P.S. Have fun and be safe<BR>||||reg||9
Z||000001||stimpy||10-28-2000||05:49 PM||||Man, that's harsh.  It's always such a tragedy to lose a friend.  We always want to make sense of these things when in reality there is often no sense to be made.  October is a bittersweet month for me as well, as tomorrow is 9 years since my older brother Russell left this earth as the result of an overdose, just 2 weeks after his 28th birthday.  I guess the only comfort is that our loved ones surely go to somewhere more comfortable then here.  That is my hope at least.||||reg||1
Z||000002||hugh||10-28-2000||07:01 PM||||I want to say something,but there is nothing anyone can say that would make either of you feel better.Time does ease the pain,but it never goes away.One thing I did to make me feel a little better after I lost my youngest brother and my father was to envision them standing on a very high cliff looking down at the world through the clouds.My father had his arm around my brother's shoulder and was saying"See,I told you they would be alright"Because no one knows when their time is up I call my wife a couple times a day and tell her I love her. ||||reg||1
Z||000003||Cliff Pennock||10-28-2000||07:12 PM||||It makes you wonder what this world is coming to. Crime seems to be on the uprise everywhere in the world and "senseless violence" seems to have become the new plague of the 21st century. Just a few weeks ago, a five year old girl was stabbed to death by her 14 year old neighbor here in Holland, because the noise she was making (outside his house) while playing, disturbed him in playing a computer game.<P>It seems to be the sign of the times. Todays youngsters seem to have no respect for authority anymore. And as controversial as it may sound, I blame today's child-protection organisations for this. They have been threathening todays parents that a good spanking or some yelling is considered child-abuse now. They want us to <B>talk</B> with our children. Stay calm and tell them what they have done wrong. Well, problem is that a 7 year old takes no message from words alone. The only message he is getting is that he can get away with stuff unpunished. My late father was pretty strict. If I did something wrong, he would spank me. If I did something good he would award me. And I believe it is this upbringing that made me the kind of person I am today; someone who knows the difference between good and bad. In today's society my father would probably be jailed for child-abuse.<P>Problem is that today's kids are tomorrow's leaders. And I shrug by the idea what kind of society that will bring.||||reg||1
Z||000004||DaRkLoRD||10-28-2000||07:28 PM||||Call me weird and over-protective.. but if/when I have kids, they're going to a private school.. a very small one.. since I grew up in a small town, violence wasn't really a problem (although one summer the gangs were so bad even the cops didn't go near them alone....)<BR>drug use and alcoholism were very common in my school.. I want to keep my kids away from all of that stuff... but it's also important to expose kids to some of the nastier things in life, such as violence, otherwise when they encounter it on their own they won't know what to do.. I haven't quite figured out what to do about that part yet.<P>------------------<BR>  <A HREF="mailto:steve@fieroproject.com">steve@fieroproject.com</A>  <BR><A HREF="http://www.fieroproject.com" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fieroproject.com</A>||||reg||9
Z||000005||Cooter||10-28-2000||08:12 PM||||To many of the kids in our society today, death has no signifigance and life has no value. In years past, when many people worked on the farm, kids would have a favorite animal that was their "pet". Then their pet would later be dinner. People from that generation knew that death has permanence, and that life was precious. I know that we can never return to simplier times, so enjoy the gift that you have- the memory of your friends and family. One day we will be united again where the sun never sets and we will never grow old. My thoughts and prayers are with you, my friends.||||reg||1
Z||000006||DaRkLoRD||10-28-2000||08:49 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Cooter:<BR><B>To many of the kids in our society today, death has no signifigance and life has no value. In years past, when many people worked on the farm, kids would have a favorite animal that was their "pet". Then their pet would later be dinner. People from that generation knew that death has permanence, and that life was precious. I know that we can never return to simplier times, so enjoy the gift that you have- the memory of your friends and family. One day we will be united again where the sun never sets and we will never grow old. My thoughts and prayers are with you, my friends.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>I don't really like the idea of eating an animal which was considered a pet.. that just seems wrong somehow.<BR>I've heard a lot of adults say kids today should be more like when they were kids, a generation earlier... yet *they* are the parents now, and they are the ones in control of society.. if kids today turned out so "wrong" compared to the adults today.. that suggests to me that the way the adults today were raised, wasn't perfect either... many kids today aren't easily intimidated, instead they react with defiance and sometimes violence towards their parents/teachers etc.. and when they have no outlet for this... they band together with other kids who feel the same, and it results in a lot of teenagers (and now some are even younger) who are so full of anger, they lash out at other kids...<P>------------------<BR>  <A HREF="mailto:steve@fieroproject.com">steve@fieroproject.com</A>  <BR><A HREF="http://www.fieroproject.com" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fieroproject.com</A>||||reg||1
Z||000007||Cheever2||10-28-2000||10:11 PM||||RiceCooker:  I can empathise.  Friend of mine (not a close friend but I dated his sister) died at his convenience store job.  Was found with an unbelievable number of stab wounds.  Was the nicest guy, no kidding.  Anyway, I didn't deal with it and hid it away.  Same happened when a much closer friend died in a helicopter crash.  It all came out last year when I realized I needed to grieve.  Man, did I grieve.  But that made all the difference to me, how I see each day that I have, and the friends I have.  You can post, and like you said, we'll read and understand.<P>Dark:  It happens in private schools too.  Kids are kids everywhere.  But those schools do have a difference in the teaching of right & wrong, like Cliff was talking about.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000008||DaRkLoRD||10-28-2000||11:06 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Cheever2:<BR><B>Dark:  It happens in private schools too.  Kids are kids everywhere.  But those schools do have a difference in the teaching of right & wrong, like Cliff was talking about.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>The kind of school I like (and wish I'd gone to) isn't very common.. closest match would be the "alternative schools". Among other things, the students would be introduced to martial arts at an early age.. this would have several benefits, such as physical fitness, but also discipline and hopefully respect. I was involved with both karate and judo for some time when I was younger, and I wish I'd started sooner and also stuck with it. I can still flip just about anyone though. hehehe..  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>------------------<BR>  <A HREF="mailto:steve@fieroproject.com">steve@fieroproject.com</A>  <BR><A HREF="http://www.fieroproject.com" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fieroproject.com</A>||||reg||1
Z||000009||Cheever2||10-28-2000||11:15 PM||||(Hoping I don't get too far from the original topic)<BR>Can't say too much, but my wife is a private school teacher, the mainstream kind, don't know for sure what you mean by alternative.  But I like what you say about martial arts.  I went to public schools while some of my friends went to private.  Went to a new school in 10th grade (1972) and wanted to be known as tough.  Lost a fight the 2nd week, so started TKD lessons.  Turned me around, man.  Had a couple judo lessons later and have an idea what you mean there too.  From a poster in my TKD school: "...for by learning to fight you actually educate yourself in the AVOIDANCE of battle".  Amen.<BR> ||||reg||1
Z||000010||JohnnyK||10-29-2000||03:12 AM||||I can sympathize. My bestbuddy died about a year and a few months ago at the HillSide festival (a few Candians will know what i'm talking about) when he drank too much and went for a swim (To get back to the island. They booted him off for rowdiness)..  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000011||AkursedX||10-29-2000||04:27 PM||||It's sad to hear all of these tragedies.  I myself have had a couple of tragedies myself these past 7 months.<BR>My best friend, first love, first everything, killed herself back in April.  Then about 3 months later another very close friend of mine killed herself.<BR>These people were such a large part of my life, and in a matter of months they were gone.  I still have yet to fully have the impact of it, but I know it will come eventually.<BR>The only thing that I really have to say is that no matter what happens in your life, try to learn something from it.  Don't let these tragedies be wasted.  Those close to us will always be in our hearts.||||reg||9
Z||000012||Monkeyman||10-29-2000||07:15 PM||||My Grandpa and I were really close.  He passed 8 years ago Halloween.  I used to enjoy Halloween, now I just think it sucks.  (Sorry.)||||reg||1
